turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+1-2 \\\\ 2+0-2 \\\\ 0+2-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=1$ and ${x}_{3}=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/state.json" "file_sha256" "eab163623da2ffe881c32948886fa8fb98a0929bc61bb83c10ac41813cb265d4"} {"question" "q-156591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c3feb68d2b84414e17c2b4f276fc60294c1532450420fca1c0db8e320007d057"} {"question" "q-156591" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/state.json" "file_sha256" "eab163623da2ffe881c32948886fa8fb98a0929bc61bb83c10ac41813cb265d4"} {"question" "q-156591" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/state.json" "file_sha256" "eab163623da2ffe881c32948886fa8fb98a0929bc61bb83c10ac41813cb265d4"} {"question" "q-156591" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/state.json" "file_sha256" "eab163623da2ffe881c32948886fa8fb98a0929bc61bb83c10ac41813cb265d4"} {"question" "q-75660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/state.json" "file_sha256" "eab163623da2ffe881c32948886fa8fb98a0929bc61bb83c10ac41813cb265d4"} {"question" "q-75660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+13{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 13 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(0)+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/state.json" "file_sha256" "eab163623da2ffe881c32948886fa8fb98a0929bc61bb83c10ac41813cb265d4"} {"question" "q-75660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c3feb68d2b84414e17c2b4f276fc60294c1532450420fca1c0db8e320007d057"} {"question" "q-75660" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/state.json" "file_sha256" "eab163623da2ffe881c32948886fa8fb98a0929bc61bb83c10ac41813cb265d4"} {"question" "q-75660" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/state.json" "file_sha256" "eab163623da2ffe881c32948886fa8fb98a0929bc61bb83c10ac41813cb265d4"} {"question" "q-75660" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/state.json" "file_sha256" "eab163623da2ffe881c32948886fa8fb98a0929bc61bb83c10ac41813cb265d4"} {"question" "q-34714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/state.json" "file_sha256" "eab163623da2ffe881c32948886fa8fb98a0929bc61bb83c10ac41813cb265d4"} {"question" "q-34714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/state.json" "file_sha256" "eab163623da2ffe881c32948886fa8fb98a0929bc61bb83c10ac41813cb265d4"} {"question" "q-34714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c3feb68d2b84414e17c2b4f276fc60294c1532450420fca1c0db8e320007d057"} {"question" "q-34714" "field" "selection" "attri